A transistor is a device made from materials that behave as semiconductors - which means they conduct electricity with minimal resistance when certain conditions are met. A typical transistor consists of a base, an emitter and a collector. The base and collector are connected to an electrical current source, and the emitter is (usually) connected to the desired input which we want to measure.

If the input is at rest then the base and emitter are both conducting and will create a circuit that allows a small amount of current to flow between them. The current flows through emitter - base - collector, and if the voltage across this circuit is smaller than the supply voltage VB you'll see a current gain, meaning the measurement of input will appear to be larger than the actual value. This effect is called amplification.

There are different types of transistors based on their behaviour when not actively receiving an input:

- Isolated field effect transistor (field effect transistor) uses a separate control electrode for eachemitter and conductor; 
- Injector junction FETs use a single collector/base electrode and have various methods of controlling electron transport betweenbase and emitter;
- Cascade MOSFETs also use one collector/base and multiple emitter electrodes but rely on electron saturation to increase output signal strength.

